  "content": {
    "text": "Tesla's (TSLA) first official Cybertruck inventory sold out in 24 hours. Barron's associate editor Al Root explains why and addresses whether the Cybertruck has any bearing on Tesla's stock.\nTesla's first official used Cybertruck inventory sells out within 24 hours. Why are these used Cybertrucks out? Why they why they selling so fast? Is this limited supply, strong demand, bit of both? Explain it for us.\nJosh, I always feel like I'm talking trucks with you. I think it's limited, I think it's limited demand. I think it's limited supply.\nUm, so it's it's a nice, it's it's good for Tesla, but uh over their entire history, I think they've sold about 60 to 70,000 of these. These would be the initial ones coming off like 2024 le- uh 24 month leases and some trade-ins. Uh so I suspect it is mostly uh low supply. However, you know, it does show that there is demand for a lower priced truck. Uh you know, the magical $50,000 electric pickup truck sort of has never arrived. Uh the mid-tier uh Cybertruck starts at 80,000, so it's still pretty much a niche luxury vehicle.\nYou're also saying in your story, Cybertruck, listen, it's fun to talk about, okay, but it it's not likely to move the stock. It's really the Cybercab, you say. That that now could be Tesla's most important model. How come, Al?\nYeah, I mean, you know, remember the good old days when Tesla was an EV company and we and we cared about sales growth and things like that. Um I'm sure process this. Now it's all about AI, right? So AI trained uh robots, robotaxis, uh FSD, their full self-driving uh consumer product, which I am a proud user of.\nUm, you know, so people really just want uh robotaxi uh progress, right? Uh and Cybercab there, it's the purpose built uh two-seater uh that uh Tesla's going to deploy in fleets and then eventually other people can buy and create their own Tesla robotaxi fleets. Um so progress on that front is really what's driving the stock. I mean, we could argue about it, but I would say at least half of the stock's valuation is in robotaxis. Um so that's, you know, we're pushing a trillion dollars for that idea. Uh so really, they just need progress on that front. You know, uh they just removed uh safety monitors in a geofenced area of Austin, uh but that entire robotaxi fleet across all of its cities is still estimated to be 40, 50 cars. It's just it's been painfully slow, but you know, investors should watch Cybercab news more than Cybertruck news.",
    "text_blocks": [
      "Tesla's (TSLA) first official Cybertruck inventory sold out in 24 hours. Barron's associate editor Al Root explains why and addresses whether the Cybertruck has any bearing on Tesla's stock.",
      "Tesla's first official used Cybertruck inventory sells out within 24 hours. Why are these used Cybertrucks out? Why they why they selling so fast? Is this limited supply, strong demand, bit of both? Explain it for us.",
      "Josh, I always feel like I'm talking trucks with you. I think it's limited, I think it's limited demand. I think it's limited supply.",
      "Um, so it's it's a nice, it's it's good for Tesla, but uh over their entire history, I think they've sold about 60 to 70,000 of these. These would be the initial ones coming off like 2024 le- uh 24 month leases and some trade-ins. Uh so I suspect it is mostly uh low supply. However, you know, it does show that there is demand for a lower priced truck. Uh you know, the magical $50,000 electric pickup truck sort of has never arrived. Uh the mid-tier uh Cybertruck starts at 80,000, so it's still pretty much a niche luxury vehicle.",
      "You're also saying in your story, Cybertruck, listen, it's fun to talk about, okay, but it it's not likely to move the stock. It's really the Cybercab, you say. That that now could be Tesla's most important model. How come, Al?",
      "Yeah, I mean, you know, remember the good old days when Tesla was an EV company and we and we cared about sales growth and things like that. Um I'm sure process this. Now it's all about AI, right? So AI trained uh robots, robotaxis, uh FSD, their full self-driving uh consumer product, which I am a proud user of.",
      "Um, you know, so people really just want uh robotaxi uh progress, right? Uh and Cybercab there, it's the purpose built uh two-seater uh that uh Tesla's going to deploy in fleets and then eventually other people can buy and create their own Tesla robotaxi fleets. Um so progress on that front is really what's driving the stock. I mean, we could argue about it, but I would say at least half of the stock's valuation is in robotaxis. Um so that's, you know, we're pushing a trillion dollars for that idea. Uh so really, they just need progress on that front. You know, uh they just removed uh safety monitors in a geofenced area of Austin, uh but that entire robotaxi fleet across all of its cities is still estimated to be 40, 50 cars. It's just it's been painfully slow, but you know, investors should watch Cybercab news more than Cybertruck news."
